WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.647 --> 00:00:13.049
was practicing her pitching at the baseball field.

2
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.352
She reached into her bucket for another ball.

3
00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:20.135
Suddenly—beep, beep, beep!

4
00:00:21.636 --> 00:00:24.238
A big truck backed slowly onto the field.

5
00:00:24.258 --> 00:00:26.700
"Hey!" Kaitlyn thought.

6
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.442
"What's that doing here?"

7
00:00:29.543 --> 00:00:30.864
A man got out of the truck.

8
00:00:32.964 --> 00:00:34.205
"Sorry," he said.

9
00:00:34.225 --> 00:00:38.128
"We need to set up here for tonight's fireworks display.

10
00:00:39.009 --> 00:00:40.510
You'll have to move somewhere else."

11
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:45.794
"The Fourth of July show is still hours away," Kaitlyn said.

12
00:00:45.814 --> 00:00:49.236
"Can't I practice my pitching some more?"

13
00:00:49.256 --> 00:00:52.199
"I'm afraid not," the man said.

14
00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:56.182
"It takes several hours to set up a fireworks display."

15
00:00:57.182 --> 00:00:59.024
By now more men had arrived.

16
00:01:00.108 --> 00:01:03.109
They were moving wooden racks and boxes onto the field.

17
00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:04.850
Kaitlyn sighed.

18
00:01:06.911 --> 00:01:08.472
She looked closely at a rack.

19
00:01:09.412 --> 00:01:11.333
It held short plastic pipes.

20
00:01:12.193 --> 00:01:13.313
"Those are fireworks?"

21
00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.095
"Nope," the man said.

22
00:01:16.115 --> 00:01:19.936
"Those are cannons that shoot the fireworks into the sky."

23
00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:24.618
He opened a box and took out a brown object wrapped in plastic.

24
00:01:25.458 --> 00:01:28.700
It was about the size of a baseball and looked like a little bomb.

25
00:01:29.592 --> 00:01:31.413
"This is a firework shell."

26
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:33.554
"What's inside of it?"

27
00:01:34.014 --> 00:01:34.814
Kaitlyn asked.

28
00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:37.676
"I'll explain," said the man.

29
00:01:38.336 --> 00:01:40.697
He set the shell gently back into the box.

30
00:01:41.738 --> 00:01:44.939
Then he bent down and drew a big circle in the dirt.

31
00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.101
"A shell is filled with little balls of gunpowder.

32
00:01:51.382 --> 00:01:55.404
Pyrotechnicians, like me, call these balls 'stars.'"

33
00:01:56.192 --> 00:02:00.513
He picked up some baseballs and formed a pattern inside the circle with them.

34
00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:04.535
"The pattern of the stars gives a firework its shape."

35
00:02:05.715 --> 00:02:07.596
"What gives a firework its color?"

36
00:02:08.016 --> 00:02:08.816
Kaitlyn asked.

37
00:02:08.836 --> 00:02:12.657
"Magic!" The man laughed.

38
00:02:14.157 --> 00:02:14.958
Kaitlyn didn't.

39
00:02:15.738 --> 00:02:17.418
She really wanted to know the answer!

40
00:02:17.438 --> 00:02:22.080
"Well, the magic of science," the man added quickly.

41
00:02:22.828 --> 00:02:26.450
"Pyrotechnicians add chemicals to the gunpowder stars.

42
00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:31.072
When these chemicals burn, they make different colors.

43
00:02:31.872 --> 00:02:37.014
Like chemicals containing copper, for example—can you guess what color they make when they burn?"

44
00:02:38.114 --> 00:02:41.196
Kaitlyn pulled a penny from her pocket and glanced at it.

45
00:02:41.216 --> 00:02:43.077
"Orangish brown?"

46
00:02:43.097 --> 00:02:45.678
"Nope!" the man said.

47
00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.959
"Chemicals with copper make blue.

48
00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:50.860
Chemicals with calcium make orange."

49
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.522
Next the man drew a square attached to the circle.

50
00:02:54.542 --> 00:02:58.684
"There's even more gunpowder here at the bottom of the shell."

51
00:02:59.765 --> 00:03:02.326
He drew a long line from the center of the circle.

52
00:03:03.186 --> 00:03:04.447
"This is the fuse.

53
00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:07.069
It's a string that lights the shell.

54
00:03:07.669 --> 00:03:12.652
When the shell is in the cannon, the fuse lights the gunpowder at the bottom of the shell.

55
00:03:13.532 --> 00:03:17.794
That explosion pushes the shell out of the cannon and into the air."

56
00:03:17.814 --> 00:03:20.696
"The shell doesn't burst inside the cannon?"

57
00:03:21.552 --> 00:03:24.473
"Nope. The fuse continues to burn in the air.

58
00:03:25.533 --> 00:03:29.234
Finally it explodes the shell and lights the stars.

59
00:03:29.894 --> 00:03:32.315
That's when you see the firework in the sky."

60
00:03:32.335 --> 00:03:35.396
"Awesome," Kaitlyn said.

61
00:03:35.416 --> 00:03:40.057
"Hmm. I guess you do have a lot to do before tonight."

62
00:03:41.057 --> 00:03:43.938
"Right. It's a big show with many fireworks!

63
00:03:44.978 --> 00:03:47.499
And we have to work carefully and safely.

64
00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:50.880
Working with fireworks is very dangerous."

65
00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.042
The man helped Kaitlyn gather her baseballs.

66
00:03:54.062 --> 00:03:56.643
"Are you going to watch the display tonight?"

67
00:03:57.043 --> 00:04:00.045
he asked, dropping the last baseball into her bucket.

68
00:04:00.786 --> 00:04:01.826
Kaitlyn smiled.

69
00:04:01.846 --> 00:04:03.667
"I can't wait!"
